    District Profile
    The facilitators collected the following secondary information about the district from the
    provincial authorities and presented it to the participants for review, the validity of which was
    subsequently confirmed by the participants:
    General Information
    Population (CSO 2005) 235,590
    Area (AIMS) 4,564 sq. km.
    Number of Community 25
    Development Councils (CDCs)
    Average land ownership One jerib (1/5 hectare) per family
    Number of vulnerable families 822 of various vulnerabilities
    Ethnic diversity 90% Pashtun, 10% Turkmen and
    Hazara
    Sectoral Information
    Number of primary schools 10
    Number of secondary schools 8
    Number of health centres 2 Basic Health Centres (BHCs), one
    Comprehensive Health Centres
    (CHC), 25 community health posts
    Core Problem and its Causes
    The participants used the “Problem Tree” analytical methodology to identify the core
    development problem and its underlying causes in the district as follows:
    Nad Ali District residents suffer from very weak local economy and extreme poverty due
    largely to reduction in agricultural and livestock productions, lack of adequate access to basic
    infrastructure and social services, and a weak local Government administration.
    Majority of district residents are engaged in agricultural and livestock productions, however,
    production levels and quality are low due to lack of access to modern agricultural methods such
    as agricultural mechanisation equipment, improved seeds and chemical fertilisers. Local
    irrigation systems, canals, karizes, brooks and streams have largely been destroyed or
    deteriorated and require restoration. The district also lacks adequate roads to facilitate trade,
    cold storages for longer preservation of agricultural productions, crop processing centres, and a
    trade centre and profitable marketing systems for locally produced goods.
    Furthermore, the area lacks reliable and affordable sources of public electricity, local economic
    development programmes and adequate social services such as a standardised education and
    quality healthcare system. The education system has extreme shortage of qualified teachers and
    education personnel, equipment and teaching materials. Many schools have been closed and
    sufficient enrolment hindered due to insecurity in the area. Moreover, district residents have
    limited access to basic health centres. The existing health centres has shortage of professional
    doctors, essential medical equipment, supplies and quality medicines, hence, residents are
    compelled to take their patients to neighbouring provinces and countries at very high costs. The
    local Government has failed to impose law and order, reduce administrative corruption in
    public offices, establish additional health centres, and construct production centres and
    establish vocational and technical training centres in the area to create employment and
    economic regeneration opportunities for local vulnerable groups such as widows, the disabled,
    the youth and returnees.
